A||||7||G-Nasty||Koni front shock adjust||||11||
Z||000000||G-Nasty||10-26-2001||02:41 AM||||I've had a pair of front Koni shock absorbers that I dont know how to adjust. The rear struts have a "key" and seperate distinct settings but as for the front?<BR>Any instructions will be preciated'&gt;||||reg||11
Z||000001||Phil||10-26-2001||07:45 AM||||You have to take the shock out and fully compress it and then turn it while compressed. Koni gets lots of calls asking that very question(don't ask me how I know).||||reg||1
Z||000002||Nashco||10-26-2001||09:28 AM||||I wondered the same thing.  I got the Koni's for when my entire suspension is rebuilt, but as it is now I just needed the front rebuilt (rear is coming up soon).  I figured it was a matter of turning the shaft...so which way is soft and hard?  Can we follow the old saying "lefty loosy, righty tighty?"  Also, does anybody know if they bottom out in either direction?  I would like to run them all the way out and then tune them by matching the number of turns in.  I will probably run them all the way hard, and just soften it up if I want to; I just want to make sure I'll be able to run them all the way down, or if they just keep spinning without any positive stops.  Help...?<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000003||Phil||10-26-2001||10:49 AM||||Definite stops and it's written on the shock soft&lt; firm&gt; or something like that||||reg||1
Z||000004||TRiAD||10-26-2001||11:15 AM||||The Konis I had on my VR6 were adjusted just like the rears, with a key, and no definitive "stops" at the settings. <P>Apparently the Fiero fronts are different. <P>------------------<BR><B><I>Michael ~ triadtuning@hotmail.com</I></B><BR>-----------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88FieroGTwhitefscustomcroppedmini.jpg"> <BR><I>Photoshop rendering of planned exterior modifications.</I>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Master_Sushi||10-26-2001||11:39 AM||||You have to take the shocks off to adjust them? I was thinking of upgrading mine. But sounds like to much of a PITA to bother.||||reg||1
Z||000006||DRH||10-26-2001||02:39 PM||||They have to be all the way in to adjust. There is a definite stop each direction. I think they have about 2 or 3 turns of adjustment if I remember right. You can tell full hard from full soft because the rod will take FOREVER to come back out on full hard.<P>IMO, you will want to stay toward the softer end. Full hard is way too stiff for the light front end. I had mine at full soft and went in about half a turn. I'm going back to full soft when I get around to taking them off again. Oh, mine is a Formula... I'm not sure if the same would apply to 84-87.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||10-26-2001||03:24 PM|||| <A HREF="http://www.koni.com" TARGET=_blank>www.koni.com</A>  has instructions for every method of adjustment they use. The instructions vary depending on exactly which shock strut is involved.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
